<?xml version="1.0" encoding="UTF-8"?>
<!--
  Proposal 44 C8: THE POSE REGENERATES WHEN A WARM STORE HAS NONE.

  THE DEFECT THIS GATES SHIPPED IN C5 AND WAS INVISIBLE THERE. The plant runs
  on the ANALYSIS side (that is where the units' couplings are), and a warm
  sidecar store is precisely what SKIPS the analysis. So a store holding valid
  groove aspects but no "body.pose" never produced one: `sFeelFlowPoseAt` fell
  back to the M3e direct mapping, silently and permanently, and only a cleared
  cache would ever have shown otherwise.

  Two populations are in exactly that shape:
    * every store written before C5 existed, and
    * EVERY store at all, the first time BodyPoseVersion is bumped, which C8
      does, because the coupled chain makes every angle a different number.

  It was found by bumping that version and watching feel_flow_puppet pass COLD
  and fail WARM, which is the one difference no single run can show.

  THE SHAPE IS BUILT, NOT WAITED FOR. `sidecar-drop` removes just the pose
  sidecar; then the track is removed and rebuilt over the SAME audio, so the
  new SFeelFlowTrackBounce has no in-memory result and its analysis hits the
  warm store. Clearing the whole store instead would make the run cold and test
  nothing; a staling EDIT would change the audio and mint a different content
  hash, which is also not this shape.

  WHAT SEPARATES PASS FROM FAIL IS THE PINNED LINE, NOT A FLOOR. The fallback
  produces LARGER numbers than the plant (about 1.09 against 0.10 summed), so a
  minAbsSum passes either way, the same trap feel_flow_trained.qxa records.
  The line below is the plant's, byte for byte the one feel_flow_puppet pins.
-->
<SActionScript version="1">
  <actions>
    <sidecar-root/>
    <add-track index="-1"/>
    <add-sample trackPath="0" filePath="../groove/a_offset15.wav" timePos="0"/>

    <!-- (1) COLD: the plant runs on the analysis side and stores its pose. -->
    <feel-flow-analyze target="track" trackPath="0"/>
    <wait-analysis timeoutMs="60000"/>
    <assert-groove-aspect aspect="body.pose" expectExists="true" minRecords="1500"/>
    <assert-feel-flow-pose trackPath="0" frame="240000" valid="1"
                           contains="pose: valid=1 bounceY=0.0015 sway=-0.0519 armSwing=0.0052 headNod=-0.0073 hipShift=0.0339 energySum=1.3862"/>

    <!-- (2) Make the store look like a pre-C5 one: groove aspects intact,
             body.pose gone. expectDropped is not pinned to a number because
             the count depends on how many keys the run above minted. -->
    <sidecar-drop aspect="body.pose"/>
    <assert-groove-aspect aspect="body.pose" expectExists="false"/>
    <assert-groove-aspect aspect="groove.dyn" expectExists="true" minRecords="1500"/>

    <!-- (3) A FRESH TRACK over the SAME audio: no in-memory result, so the
             analysis takes the warm-store path and never runs the plant. -->
    <remove-track trackPath="0"/>
    <add-track index="-1"/>
    <add-sample trackPath="0" filePath="../groove/a_offset15.wav" timePos="0"/>
    <feel-flow-analyze target="track" trackPath="0"/>
    <wait-analysis timeoutMs="60000"/>

    <!-- (4) The pose must be the PLANT's, identical to (1), which means the
             read side regenerated it from the loaded dynamics and the
             ensemble's own coupling constants. On the pre-fix binary this
             reads the fallback's numbers instead. -->
    <assert-feel-flow-pose trackPath="0" frame="240000" valid="1"
                           contains="pose: valid=1 bounceY=0.0015 sway=-0.0519 armSwing=0.0052 headNod=-0.0073 hipShift=0.0339 energySum=1.3862"/>

    <!-- (5) And it was WRITTEN BACK, so the next process does not pay for it
             again. This is the half that makes the fix a repair rather than a
             per-run workaround. -->
    <assert-groove-aspect aspect="body.pose" expectExists="true" minRecords="1500"/>
  </actions>

  <assertions>
    <assert-track-count equals="1"/>
  </assertions>

</SActionScript>
